      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To study the effects of preoperative phenoxybenzamine (o.p) on preventing epidural-morphine-induced urinary retention and effects of neostigmine on releasing it."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "80 patients who received epidural morphine to release postoperative pain were divided into 4 groups randomly and received preoperative phenoxybenzamine (group P, n = 20), neostigmine (i. m) when urinary retention happened (group N, n = 20), both drugs (group U, n = 20), and without drugs (Control, n = 20), respectively. The incidences of urinary retention and release were observed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "No significant decrease (P > 0.05) happened after operation in the incidence of epidural-morphine-induced urinary retention both in the group P and in the group N. Neostigmine(i. m) could significantly release (P < 0.001) the epidural-morphine-induced urinary retention when phenoxybenzamine was administrated preoperatively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Preoperative phenoxybenzamine can not prevent epidural-morphine-induced urinary retention. Neostigmine can release the urinary retention only when preoperative phenoxybenzamine is used."
        }
      ]},
